10:22:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Rainbows are not unique to sunlight. Any sufficiently bright light shining on water droplets can make one. You can sometimes see artificial rainbows in sprinklers, fountains, mist, or rain illuminated by car headlights or floodlights. Moonbows are the same effect from moonlight, though they often look nearly white because they are dim. The Sun is simply the best and most common source because it has the right properties: - **Very bright:** A rainbow’s light is spread out over a large area, so the source needs to be strong. Most street lamps are too dim compared with daylight. - **Broad spectrum (“white” light):** Sunlight contains all visible colors, which droplets separate into red, orange, yellow, etc. Some lamps—especially older sodium streetlights—produce mostly one or a few colors, so they cannot make a vivid multicolored rainbow. - **Effectively a distant point source:** The Sun is so far away that its rays arrive almost parallel. This produces the familiar circular arc, centered about 42° away from the direction opposite the Sun. Nearby or large lamps send light from many directions, blurring or distorting the pattern. - **Usually viewed against a dark background:** With the Sun behind you and rain ahead, the sky behind the rainbow can be dark enough for it to stand out. Artificial lights often illuminate the surroundings and reduce contrast. So street lamps can produce rainbow-like effects, but they are usually faint, blurred, oddly shaped, or lacking strong colors.
