10:22:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that, in ordinary life, **recognizable artificial rainbows are much rarer than the simple “any light can make one” statement suggests**. A rainbow needs more than light and water droplets. The geometry is very strict: - The light must be **behind you**. - The droplets must be in front of you at about **40–42° from the direction opposite the light**. - A large enough region of droplets must be illuminated from nearly the **same direction**. - The light must be bright and broad-spectrum enough for the separated colors to be visible. The Sun meets all of these conditions almost perfectly and automatically. It is extremely bright, its light is essentially parallel across an entire rain shower, and it illuminates a huge volume of droplets uniformly. That lets all the droplets contributing to the same 42° viewing angle line up into a large, sharp arc. Streetlights and building lights usually fail in several ways at once: 1. **They are much too dim compared with the Sun.** Direct sunlight is roughly 100,000 lux. Even strong floodlighting is typically hundreds or a few thousand lux where it reaches the rain, often much less. Rainbow light is only a small fraction of the light entering each droplet, so it gets faint fast. 2. **They are too close and too extended.** A lamp’s rays spread outward from a nearby source rather than arriving parallel. For droplets at different distances and positions, the required rainbow angle points in different directions. Instead of one clean, coherent bow, the colors smear out. 3. **The lamp is usually in the wrong place.** If you are looking at floodlit rain, the floodlight is often beside you or in front of you. But the rainbow-producing light is sent back toward the observer, so the source generally needs to be behind the observer—like the Sun is when you see a normal rainbow. 4. **Fixtures often do not illuminate the needed part of the sky or mist.** A street lamp shines mostly downward, often with shielding. The droplets in the correct 42° direction may simply not be lit. 5. **The spectrum may be poor.** Old sodium streetlights are nearly yellow-orange, so there is little color range to separate. Modern white LEDs are better, but their spectrum is still not as smooth and broad as sunlight. So artificial rainbows are physically possible, but a full “outdoor sky rainbow” requires an unusually favorable setup: a very bright, relatively small white light source behind and near the observer, a broad curtain of fine mist ahead, darkness around it, and the right viewing angle. Those conditions are routine with sunlight after rain, but almost never happen accidentally with streetlights or building illumination.
